.TH mbwcdview 1 "1 November 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbwcdview\fP \- Display water column data, one swath at a time.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbwcdview\fP
.br
[
.br
\fB\-\-input\fP=\fIfile\fP {\fB\-I\fP\fIfile\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-format\fP=\fIformat_id\fP {\fB\-F\fP\fIformat_id\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-ping\fP=\fIindex\fP {\fB\-P\fP\fIindex\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-swath\fP=\fIindex\fP {\fB\-S\fP\fIindex\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-bottom\fP
.br
\fB\-\-wc-bottom\fP
.br
\fB\-\-straight\fP
.br
\fB\-\-export\fP=\fIimage.png\fP
.br
\fB\-\-size\fP=\fIwidth\fPx\fIheight\fP
.br
\fB\-\-dump\fP
.br
\fB\-\-verbose\fP {\fB\-V\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-help\fP {\fB\-H\fP}
.br
]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbwcdview\fP is a Qt6 program that displays the water column data of swath
sonar files, read through the MBIO library, as an image of the beamformed
amplitudes against across track distance and depth, one swath at a time. Water
column data are available for the Kongsberg kmall format (format 261), either
in the separate *.kmwcd files (water column only) or in the *.kmall files. The
original files are read; no preprocessing is needed.

When a Kongsberg *.kmwcd or *.kmall file is opened, \fBmbwcdview\fP looks for the
other file of the same survey line in the same directory: the same line number
(the first four characters of the file name) and the nearest time in the file
name. Kongsberg SIS names each file after the time of its first datagram, so the
two names may differ by seconds. The water column pings are matched with the
survey pings of the *.kmall by ping number and time. The *.kmall supplies the
navigation, the vessel heading, the transducer depth and the soundings; the
*.kmwcd supplies the water column. Any of these may be missing: without a *.kmall
the navigation is taken from the position records of the *.kmwcd, if present,
and depths are given below the transducer.

The water column samples are ray traced through the sound velocity profile
logged with the data (\fBmb_rt\fP(3), as \fBmbprocess\fP traces soundings), from
the transducer depth and with the beam angles and the sound speed at the
transducer logged with the water column. The soundings then lie on the seafloor
echo. With the \fBRay trace (SVP)\fP check box off (or \fB\-\-straight\fP) the
samples are placed along straight rays with the sound speed at the transducer;
in deep water with a fast surface layer this puts the seafloor echo noticeably
deeper than the soundings and the outer beams tens of metres off. No motion
during the ping (heave, pitch, roll) is applied.

The amplitudes are shown in dB. For Kongsberg data these are the logged values
(0.5 dB steps) minus the gain offset (TVGoffset_dB), the display value defined by
Kongsberg. The lowest logged value (\-128, i.e. \-94 dB with a 30 dB offset) is a
fill value, for example for near field samples, and is drawn as no data.

The window has controls for the depth range, the amplitude range of the colour
scale, a fixed across track scale, the display of the soundings of the matching
*.kmall ping (\fBBottom\fP: red amplitude, dark blue phase detections, filled good,
hollow flagged) and of the bottom detections recorded in the water column itself
(\fBWC bottom\fP, orange line), a slider (also mouse wheel and the left and right
arrow keys, one swath per step), a replay at a given rate, saving the image at
twice the screen resolution with the file name and ping information, and
picking positions in the image (\fBPick\fP) into a table that can be saved as text.
The image is drawn with OpenGL, so stepping through the pings is fast.

Without a window, \fB\-\-dump\fP prints a summary of a survey line and
\fB\-\-export\fP writes an image of one swath.

